"""Detailed continuation: methods, measured results and their direct limits."""
import numpy as np
from reportlab.lib.units import mm
from production.remaining_evidence import FAMILIES,LABELS,FEATURES,TARGETS
from production.report_equations import equation


def question(a,text):a.p('<font color="red">[Jason: '+text+']</font>')
def number(x,n=3):return f'{float(x):.{n}f}'
def target_name(t):
    f,s=t.rsplit('_',1)
    return FEATURES[f]+' ('+s+')'


def write_remaining(a,assets,e):
    fingerprints(a,assets,e)
    timing(a,assets,e)
    identification_and_probes(a,assets,e)
    conditioning(a,assets,e)
    components(a,assets,e)
    events(a,assets,e)
    dashboard(a,assets,e)


def fingerprints(a,assets,e):
    a.page();a.h('6 Testing the contribution of the personal fingerprint')
    a.p('The preceding experiment used a participant\'s context trials to generate a distribution for that participant. To establish whether the personal code contributed useful information beyond the shared decoder, we repeated generation after replacing only the center of the latent sampling distribution. This control separates the value of personal enrollment from the model\'s general ability to reproduce typical movements.')
    a.h('6.1 Fingerprint replacement within the same decoder',True)
    a.p('Three alternatives were evaluated: the participant\'s own context centroid; the equal-person mean of the 17 training participants\' context centroids; and each of the six other test participants\' context centroids. For a given participant and checkpoint, the decoder, shared covariance, 120 latent-noise draws, task-condition draws and training feature-distance reference were held fixed. The other-person result was the mean of six separately calculated distances. Their samples were not pooled into one mixture.')
    a.p('The control was run for all CVAE checkpoints at n=3 and n=8 (four folds and three seeds), and subsequently for all 12 VAE n=8 checkpoints. Each own-centroid arm reproduced its original generation distances before the substitutions were compared. Distances were averaged over training seeds within each participant and then across the 28 participants.')
    rows=[['Model','Fingerprint center','Mean KS','Energy','MMD squared']]
    for family,dim in [('cvae',3),('cvae',8),('unconditional_vae',8)]:
        frame=e['all_fingerprint'].query('model_family == @family and latent_dim == @dim').set_index('arm')
        for arm,label in [('own','Own participant'),('population','Training-population mean'),('wrong','Other test participants')]:
            r=frame.loc[arm];rows.append([f'{LABELS[family]} n={dim}',label,*[number(r[k]) for k in ['mean_ks','energy_distance','mmd_rbf']]])
    a.table(rows,'<b>Table 5.</b> Fingerprint replacement with the generative procedure held fixed. Lower discrepancies are better. The other-person row averages donor-specific distances.',[30*mm,55*mm,27*mm,27*mm,27*mm])
    a.h('6.2 Results and statistical scope',True)
    a.p(f'Own-context generation had lower mean discrepancies than both controls in every model/capacity comparison in Table 5. The 12 CVAE contrasts used two-sided paired Pratt Wilcoxon tests and one Benjamini-Hochberg adjustment family; the largest adjusted p-value was {e["fingerprint_paired"].p_fdr_bh.max():.6f}. The six VAE n=8 contrasts formed a separate follow-up family; all adjusted p-values were at most {e["uvae_fp_paired"].q_bh_6.max():.2g}. Both analyses used seed-averaged participant values and differences rounded to 12 decimals before ranking.')
    a.p('At CVAE n=3, the own fingerprint improved mean KS for 22 of 28 participants against the population center and 27 of 28 against other-person centers. At CVAE n=8 and VAE n=8, the corresponding counts were 27 of 28 for each control. These results support useful personal information within the recorded session. They do not imply that every participant improves or that every generated feature distribution is accurate.')
    a.page();a.h('6.3 Participant-level effects',True)
    a.figure(assets/'fingerprint_controls.png','<b>Figure 11.</b> Change in mean KS produced by replacing a control center with the participant\'s own center. Positive values favor the own fingerprint. Each point is one participant after seed averaging; black bars mark means. CVAE n=3 represents the compact setting and VAE n=8 the dashboard default.',83*mm)
    a.p('The pattern is compatible with a compact representation retaining useful person-related variation. It does not by itself establish a persistent trait: enrollment and evaluation draw from different trials within the same available session. The comparison also remains dependent on the shared decoder and covariance. There was no separate session in which a previously estimated fingerprint was tested unchanged.')
    a.p('These were follow-up controls on the evaluated cohort. Fold training sets overlap, and the VAE follow-up was motivated by its observed generation performance. The adjusted p-values describe these specified comparisons; they do not provide an independent validation of model selection or a physiological interpretation of the coordinates.')
    question(a,'Is within-session distribution modeling the intended first target, and are repeat sessions available for testing whether a participant fingerprint remains useful across sessions?')


def timing(a,assets,e):
    a.page();a.h('7 Recovering physical timing from normalized trajectory shape')
    a.p('Resampling removes absolute duration from the trajectory input, although relative waiting and path progression remain visible. Timing prediction therefore tested whether the normalized path retained information about when movement began and how long it lasted. In this experiment the model received the complete observed test trajectory, rather than only a participant centroid. It was not forecasting the duration of a future trial before its trajectory was observed.')
    a.p('The neural decoders predicted two standardized log-durations alongside the coordinates. Spline + PCA used its separate Ridge timing model. Predictions were inverted to seconds and compared with durations derived from the recorded indices. Absolute errors were averaged within participants, then over neural seeds and participants; Table 6 reports milliseconds.')
    equation(a,assets,'mae')
    rows=[['Model','n','Initiation MAE (ms)','Movement MAE (ms)']]
    for n in [3,8]:
        for f in FAMILIES:
            r=e['means'].query('model_family == @f and latent_dim == @n').iloc[0]
            rows.append([LABELS[f],n,number(r.initiation_time_mae_ms,2),number(r.movement_time_mae_ms,2)])
    r=e['means'].query('model_family == "condition_ridge"').iloc[0]
    rows.append(['Condition-only Ridge','-',number(r.initiation_time_mae_ms,2),number(r.movement_time_mae_ms,2)])
    a.table(rows,'<b>Table 6.</b> Original timing-head errors. Physical durations were withheld from trajectory encoders. Lower participant-balanced MAE is better.',[50*mm,12*mm,52*mm,52*mm])
    a.p('Spline + PCA\'s reconstruction advantage did not extend to its original timing head. All three neural families had smaller mean timing errors at both matched capacities. Among neural models, the lowest mean initiation error was CAE at n=3 and CVAE at n=8; VAE had the lowest mean movement-duration error at both capacities. These numerical rankings do not constitute a single overall model ranking.')
    a.h('7.1 Tail errors and the limits of an average',True)
    a.p('Mean absolute error does not reveal every extreme prediction. The saved CVAE n=8, seed-43 initiation predictions included an absolute error of 11.546 s, despite a cohort-level MAE on the order of tens of milliseconds. The inverse logarithmic transform is unbounded above. A one-second flag was used as a diagnostic threshold, not as a validated physiological cutoff or a rule for removing observations. Large errors remained in the reported scores.')
    question(a,'What timing ranges are plausible for this task, and should a future model constrain durations explicitly rather than only flagging unusually large predictions?')
    a.page();a.h('7.2 Timing-head sensitivity comparisons',True)
    a.p('The original heads differed: the CVAE used a nonlinear neural timing decoder, whereas spline used linear Ridge. To examine this difference, the same two-hidden-layer MLP (64 ReLU units per layer) was fitted to each frozen representation plus its five task values. Training-only standardization was applied to codes/conditions and log-duration targets. The head used Adam at 0.001, batch size 64, L2 coefficient 0.0001 and inverse-trial-count participant weights.')
    a.p('Validation selected the checkpoint using weighted standardized log-duration MSE, with an improvement tolerance of 1e-7, patience 40 and at most 400 passes. Four folds, two capacities, two representations and three seeds produced 48 small timing-head fits. Neither neural representation was retrained. In a separate sensitivity, each original timing output was multiplied by a validation-fitted factor minimizing participant-weighted absolute error in seconds. This calibration was then applied unchanged to test predictions.')
    rows=[['Head','n','Duration','CVAE MAE','Spline MAE','BH-adjusted p']]
    for _,r in e['timing_paired'].iterrows():
        rows.append(['Common MLP' if r.comparison=='common_mlp' else 'Calibrated original',int(r.latent_dim),r.endpoint,number(r.cvae_mean_ms,2),number(r.spline_mean_ms,2),f'{r.p_fdr_bh:.4g}'])
    a.table(rows,'<b>Table 7.</b> Timing sensitivities in ms. BH adjustment covers the eight paired participant comparisons shown. These saved sensitivity tests use Pratt handling without the explicit 12-decimal rounding used in the main comparison.',[36*mm,10*mm,29*mm,28*mm,28*mm,35*mm])
    a.figure(assets/'timing_heads.png','<b>Figure 12.</b> Timing errors with original, common-MLP and validation-calibrated heads. Points represent separate fitted procedures; lines connect them to make the comparison readable.',79*mm)
    a.p('With the common MLP, CVAE retained lower initiation MAE after the eight-test correction, while movement-time differences did not survive correction. Calibration retained CVAE\'s advantage over spline on both durations. These checks do not isolate a pure representation effect: CVAE codes had been learned with timing supervision, while spline PCA had been fitted to shape coefficients. They also do not show that calibration is uniformly beneficial or establish the cause of the original gap.')


def identification_and_probes(a,assets,e):
    a.page();a.h('8 Participant separation and behavioural information in the code')
    a.p('The replacement control establishes that the personal centroid matters to generation. Two further analyses asked what information could be read from the latent representation: whether query trials could be assigned to the correct enrolled participant, and whether a participant\'s code predicted specific behavioural summaries.')
    a.h('8.1 Closed-set participant enrollment',True)
    a.p('Within each outer test fold, all seven test participants were enrolled using their context centroids. Each query trial was assigned to the nearest centroid by Euclidean distance after dividing each coordinate by its standard deviation over the pooled test-context codes (plus 1e-8). Query codes did not determine this scale. Balanced accuracy averaged recall across the seven participants, and results were averaged across folds and training seeds. The reference for uniform guessing was 1/7, or 14.3%.')
    rows=[['Model','n=3 balanced accuracy','n=8 balanced accuracy']]
    for f in FAMILIES:
        g=e['model_comparison'].query('model_family == @f').set_index('latent_dim')
        rows.append([LABELS[f],*[f'{100*g.loc[n,"fingerprint_balanced_accuracy_mean"]:.1f}%' for n in [3,8]]])
    a.table(rows,'<b>Table 8.</b> Identification among seven enrolled test participants. These are descriptive closed-set results, not identification of previously unseen identities without enrollment.',[58*mm,54*mm,54*mm])
    a.p('Accuracy exceeded the 14.3% reference numerically for every listed setting. At n=8 the two variational models were near 51%, while at n=3 they were near 40%. This result indicates partial separation of participant-related variation, with considerable overlap. It is not evidence for perfectly separated clusters or stability across sessions.')
    a.figure(assets/'latent_three_dimensions.png','<b>Figure 13.</b> Three-dimensional query codes (small points) and context centroids (crosses) for all seven test participants in fold 0, seed 42. Each panel uses one fitted coordinate system. Axes from separately fitted models are not aligned; the viewing angle and point selection do not optimize apparent separation.',86*mm)
    a.page();a.h('8.2 Earlier clustering diagnostic',True)
    a.p('An earlier descriptive analysis standardized the complete trajectory matrix or a selected kinematic-feature matrix over all available trials, then applied K-means with 28 clusters, ten initializations and seed 42. Agreement with participant labels was measured by adjusted Rand index (ARI). Two hundred permutations of participant labels provided a reference for each fixed clustering; the p-value used a plus-one correction. This analysis was in-sample and did not use the outer model-evaluation folds.')
    rows=[['Input','ARI','Normalized mutual information','Permutation p']]
    for _,r in e['kmeans'].iterrows():rows.append([r.representation.replace('_',' '),number(r.ari,4),number(r.nmi,4),number(r.permutation_p,5)])
    a.table(rows,'<b>Table 9.</b> Historical K-means diagnostic. Small ARI values indicate weak agreement with participant identity even though both exceed their permutation reference.',[57*mm,25*mm,53*mm,31*mm])
    a.p('K-means assigns observations to the nearest of 28 fitted centers; it was not given participant labels while fitting those centers. The labels were used afterward to evaluate agreement. ARI compares pairwise co-membership with chance adjustment: a value near zero indicates little agreement beyond its chance reference. Normalized mutual information measures dependence between the cluster and participant labels, but is not chance-adjusted in the same way.')
    a.p('The historical kinematic matrix used the configured clustering feature list, including endpoint z and excluding speed-peak count. It should not be confused with the later 11-feature generation endpoint. Standardization and clustering used all available trials for this descriptive analysis, whereas reconstruction and generation were assessed on participants held out from model fitting.')
    a.p('Both observed ARIs exceeded every one of the 200 shuffled-label values, giving the minimum attainable plus-one p-value of 1/201. A small permutation p-value alongside a small ARI indicates detectable but weak agreement, not clean participant clusters. Trial-label exchangeability is an assumption of this descriptive reference; repeated trials and session structure limit a broader inferential interpretation.')
    a.page();a.h('8.3 Predicting participant-level behavioural summaries',True)
    a.p('For each participant, the means and sample standard deviations of seven query features formed 14 targets: initiation time, movement time, curvature index, peak speed, path length, maximum lateral deviation and heuristic speed-peak count. A separate Ridge regression predicted each target from the context-mean latent fingerprint. Thus the predictor matrix contained one row per participant, rather than one row per trajectory.')
    a.p('Coordinate scaling was fitted on the 17 training participants. Ridge alpha was chosen from 0.01, 0.1, 1, 10 and 100 using MSE on four validation participants. After selection, the regressor was refitted on all 21 development participants while retaining the training-fitted input scale, and tested on seven participants. The encoder remained frozen. Predictions from all four test folds were pooled to score 28 participants per training seed.')
    equation(a,assets,'r2')
    a.p('Pooled R-squared used the mean of the 28 observed query-summary targets in its denominator. Zero is the error of that constant mean; negative values indicate greater squared error. This comparison constant is distinct from a deployable training-mean predictor. Averaging seven-person fold R-squared values gives a different result because each fold has its own denominator. Figure 14 reports pooled scores averaged over available model seeds and retains all negative values.')
    a.figure(assets/'probe_scores.png','<b>Figure 14.</b> All 14 behavioural-summary targets for the four matched model families. Colors saturate below -1, but annotations show the actual values. No targets are removed because their R-squared is negative. Spline has one deterministic fit per fold; neural values average three training seeds.',124*mm)
    for f,n in [('cvae',3),('unconditional_vae',8)]:
        g=e['probes'].query('model_family == @f and latent_dim == @n and fingerprint == "mean"')
        a.p(f'{LABELS[f]} n={n} had {int((g.r2_oof_mean<0).sum())} of 14 pooled scores below zero and {int((g.r2_fold_mean<0).sum())} of 14 mean-fold scores below zero. The useful personal information demonstrated by generation therefore did not translate into uniformly accurate prediction of these individual summaries.')
    a.page();a.h('8.4 Direct context measurements as a reference',True)
    a.p('The same context recordings also allow direct measurement of each target summary. We therefore compared the latent probes with copying the corresponding context mean or sample SD to predict the query summary. A further constant predictor used the equal-person average of the 21 development participants\' query summaries. These references received no test-query values. The comparison was originally tabulated for CVAE; the current report extends the same calculation to the saved CAE, VAE and spline predictions after verifying identical query targets.')
    direct=e['all_direct'];rows=[['Query-summary target','CVAE n=3','VAE n=8','Direct context','Dev. constant']]
    for target in TARGETS:
        mul=1000 if target.startswith(('initiation_time','movement_time')) else 1
        def get(f,n,arm):return direct.loc[(direct.model_family==f)&(direct.latent_dim==n)&(direct.target==target)&(direct.arm==arm)].iloc[0].mae*mul
        label=target_name(target)+(' [ms]' if mul==1000 else '')
        rows.append([label,number(get('cvae',3,'latent_probe'),3),number(get('unconditional_vae',8,'latent_probe'),3),number(get('cvae',3,'direct_context'),3),number(get('cvae',3,'development_constant'),3)])
    a.table(rows,'<b>Table 10.</b> MAE for all 14 query-summary targets, highlighting the compact CVAE and dashboard VAE. Timing is in ms; other errors use each feature\'s units. Scores average seed-specific absolute errors, not errors after averaging predictions.',[62*mm,26*mm,26*mm,26*mm,26*mm])
    a.p('Direct context measurements yielded mean-initiation R-squared of 0.912 and mean-peak-speed R-squared of 0.976, with MAEs of 9.275 ms and 1.734 tracker units/s. These descriptive references are especially relevant when the intended output is a specific summary rather than a sampled trajectory.')
    rows=[['Latent probe','n=3: targets with lower direct MAE','n=8: targets with lower direct MAE']]
    for f in FAMILIES:
        counts=[]
        for n in [3,8]:
            g=direct.query('model_family == @f and latent_dim == @n').pivot(index='target',columns='arm',values='mae')
            counts.append(f'{int((g.direct_context<g.latent_probe).sum())}/14')
        rows.append([LABELS[f],*counts])
    a.table(rows,'<b>Table 11.</b> Coverage of the direct-context advantage across all saved mean-fingerprint probes. This is a descriptive comparison without a new significance test.',[46*mm,60*mm,60*mm])
    a.p('Direct measurement preserves the particular statistic being predicted, whereas a latent code shares three or eight coordinates across all tasks. Fourteen direct summaries jointly require 14 numbers. The comparison therefore exposes a compression tradeoff; it does not test whether direct summaries can decode complete trajectories.')
    a.page();a.h('8.5 Adding latent variability to the fingerprint',True)
    a.p('An additional probe variant was saved for neural capacities n=2 and n=3. It concatenated the context mean with coordinate-wise standard deviations of the equally weighted posterior mixture. Those variances included variation between context posterior means and average posterior variance. The n=3 variant therefore used six predictor values and must not be presented as a three-number fingerprint.')
    rows=[['Query-summary target','CVAE2 mean','CVAE2 mean + SD','CVAE3 mean + SD','CAE3 mean + SD','VAE3 mean + SD']]
    scores=e['probes'].set_index(['model_family','latent_dim','fingerprint','target'])
    configs=[('cvae',2,'mean'),('cvae',2,'mean_plus_sd'),('cvae',3,'mean_plus_sd'),('conditional_ae',3,'mean_plus_sd'),('unconditional_vae',3,'mean_plus_sd')]
    for t in TARGETS:rows.append([target_name(t),*[number(scores.loc[(f,n,k,t),'r2_oof_mean'],3) for f,n,k in configs]])
    a.table(rows,'<b>Table 12.</b> Pooled R-squared for all saved mean-plus-SD probe variants. CVAE2 uses two means or four mean/SD values; the other expanded variants use six values. The corresponding n=3 mean-only scores are in Figure 14.',[56*mm,22*mm,22*mm,22*mm,22*mm,22*mm])
    a.p('At n=3, the number of targets with positive pooled R-squared changed from four to six for CVAE, four to three for CAE and five to six for VAE. These counts are descriptive and are not a combined performance score. Adding coordinate variability did not yield uniformly accurate behavioural prediction.')
    a.h('8.6 Decoder associations in the dashboard',True)
    a.p('The dashboard provides a complementary descriptive view: it samples 250 to 1,000 latent points around the training-population center with the shared covariance (sampling seed 1147), decodes them under the selected condition where supported, and computes Spearman correlations between each latent coordinate and six generated features. The features are initiation time, movement time, peak speed, path length, curvature and lateral deviation. These correlations describe co-variation under the chosen sampling distribution; they are not participant-held-out probe scores.')
    a.p('Latent-feature associations and slider traversals describe the fitted coordinate system. They do not identify independently validated strategy axes, and axes may rotate, change sign or exchange roles in another fit. The probe results above provide a separate test of predictive information instead of relying on a visually suggestive association.')


def conditioning(a,assets,e):
    a.page();a.h('9 Checking explicit task conditioning')
    a.p('The unconditional VAE\'s competitive performance raised two different questions: whether condition inputs reached the network correctly, and whether conditioning improved prediction of task-related differences. These questions require different checks. A working condition input does not guarantee lower error after finite training and validation selection.')
    a.h('9.1 Implementation and frozen-network perturbations',True)
    a.p('The audit checked all 96 saved neural checkpoints. CVAE and CAE concatenated the five-value condition vector to both encoder and decoder, and every tested condition coordinate affected their learned mappings. All 24 unconditional networks replaced these inputs by zeros and were exactly invariant to condition swaps. Enabling conditioning in a copy of each unconditional checkpoint while zeroing its condition-weight blocks preserved its outputs exactly. The conditional architecture can therefore represent the unconditional solution, although training need not find that solution.')
    a.p('A further perturbation shuffled complete valid condition tuples within each test participant using five fixed permutations. One arm retained the original latent code and changed only decoder conditions; another changed conditions at both encoder and decoder. The networks were not retrained. Table 13 reports participant-averaged trajectory reconstruction MSE under these perturbations.')
    rows=[['Model','n','Correct conditions','Decoder shuffled','Both shuffled','Correct better: decoder']]
    for f in ['cvae','conditional_ae']:
        for n in [3,8]:
            g=e['condition_shuffle'].query('family == @f and dim == @n').set_index('arm');r=g.loc['decoder_only']
            rows.append([LABELS[f],n,number(r.correct_mse,6),number(r.shuffled_mse,6),number(g.loc['both','shuffled_mse'],6),f'{int(r.correct_better_n)}/28'])
    a.table(rows,'<b>Table 13.</b> Frozen condition-shuffle diagnostic. All reported values come from the perturbation export; the last column counts participants with lower MSE under correct decoder conditions.',[24*mm,10*mm,32*mm,32*mm,32*mm,36*mm])
    a.p('Decoder-only shuffling increased mean error in all four settings. Changing both encoder and decoder produced smaller, mixed effects, including a small mean improvement for CAE n=8. Decoder-only shuffling deliberately disrupts the usual relation between latent code and condition. These findings establish sensitivity of the mappings, not a causal effect of task conditions or a controlled-generation accuracy guarantee.')
    a.h('9.2 Fidelity within task strata',True)
    a.p('A separate CVAE-VAE comparison generated 120 samples within each available starting-category by side stratum. The participant fingerprint and latent-noise draws were reused across strata; continuous executed speed was sampled from that stratum\'s query metadata. Empirical and generated mean trajectories were compared, and each feature was evaluated by KS and standardized absolute error of the median. Strata and neural seeds were averaged within participants before testing.')
    rows=[['n','CVAE median mean-path MSE','VAE median mean-path MSE','Holm-adjusted p']]
    for _,r in e['condition_trajectory'].iterrows():rows.append([int(r.latent_dim),number(r.cvae_median,4),number(r.unconditional_vae_median,4),number(r.wilcoxon_p_holm,4)])
    a.table(rows,'<b>Table 14.</b> Participant medians of condition-stratified mean-trajectory discrepancy. This is a comparison of mean generated and recorded paths, not individual-trial reconstruction. Holm adjustment covers the two capacities.',[12*mm,55*mm,55*mm,44*mm])
    a.p('Neither trajectory contrast survived the two-test adjustment, and none of 44 feature comparisons survived BH correction (two capacities, 11 features and two metrics). Category-3 minus category-1 and right-minus-left median contrasts were also summarized descriptively. Consequently, the available checks do not establish that CVAE controls task-specific distributions more accurately than VAE. They also do not establish that conditions are irrelevant to movement.')
    question(a,'Which task-specific change should controlled generation reproduce most clearly, and should that endpoint be prioritized in a future validation?')


def components(a,assets,e):
    a.page();a.h('10 Minimum-jerk components as a secondary kinematic description')
    a.p('The 11-feature evaluation includes a heuristic speed-peak count, but a local peak does not necessarily correspond to a separate movement component. To examine finer temporal structure, a secondary analysis approximated planar velocity as a sum of smooth minimum-jerk pulses. The purpose was to compare a specified kinematic description between recorded and generated movements, rather than infer decisions directly from a fitted count.')
    a.h('10.1 Pulse model and numerical fitting',True)
    a.p('Jerk is the rate of change of acceleration. The classical minimum-jerk formulation minimizes its squared magnitude integrated over a movement [6]. Our component basis used the resulting bell-shaped velocity profile, with onset t, duration T and planar displacement d for each component [7]. A component was zero outside its active interval; the fitted velocity was the vector sum of its components.')
    equation(a,assets,'mj_velocity')
    a.p('Candidates contained one to four components. Each was fitted by bounded nonlinear least squares using the trust-region reflective algorithm, Jacobian scaling and deterministic trial-specific restart seeds. The residual combined lateral velocity, forward velocity and speed, normalized by the recorded velocity energy. Speed in the prediction was the norm of the summed velocity, not the sum of component speeds.')
    equation(a,assets,'mj_error')
    a.p('In this expression, the two equal terms in the denominator arise from the planar squared norm and the squared scalar speed; the denominator was floored at 1e-12. Velocity was padded with zeros beyond the observed movement so that components extending beyond the endpoint incurred error. This is an adapted implementation of Friedman\'s repository [7], rather than byte-identical execution of its optimizer or the published scattershot algorithm [8].')
    rows=[['Setting','Implemented value or rule'],['Candidate count','One to four, subject to feasible onset bounds'],['Component duration','0.100 to 1.000 s'],['Lateral / forward displacement','[-5, 5] / [0, 20] tracker units'],['Onset lower bounds','Component index times 0.050 s (index begins at zero)'],['Onset upper bound','max(movement duration - 0.050 s, 0.050 s)'],['Matched fitting budget','Two restarts; at most 400 function evaluations per restart'],['Primary order choice','Smallest count with E <= 0.05; otherwise smallest with E < 0.10; otherwise minimum E']]
    a.table(rows,'<b>Table 15.</b> Component fitting assumptions. The onset lower bounds are absolute component-index bounds; they do not enforce pairwise onset separation after fitting.',[49*mm,117*mm])
    a.p('An alternative count minimized the stored BIC-style criterion below, where N was three times the padded number of time points and there were four fitted parameters per component. The criterion was retained as a model-order sensitivity; its residual independence assumptions are not established for filtered velocities.')
    equation(a,assets,'bic')
    question(a,'Are this pulse basis, displacement bounds, 100-ms minimum duration, endpoint padding and order-selection rule appropriate for these interception movements, or should the decomposition follow a different lab convention?')
    a.page();a.h('10.2 Matched processing of recorded and generated inputs',True)
    a.p('The original decomposition comparison used different numerical budgets and input representations for recorded and generated movements. A matched evaluation therefore started from the same type of model-target window: 100 planar positions from target motion to the final sample. Both inputs underwent the same timing-based crop to 100 movement points, restoration of physical sampling rate as 99 divided by movement duration, low-pass filtering and component fitter.')
    a.p('The recorded input used measured timing and retained its earlier acquisition preprocessing; generated input used predicted timing. Matching the downstream operator does not remove these upstream differences. The resulting endpoint describes the model\'s filtered representation, rather than unprocessed biological components.')
    a.p('All 2,376 recorded query windows and 1,680 CVAE-generated windows were fitted: ten generated trials per participant, capacity and neural seed at n=3 and n=8. The VAE n=8 follow-up added 840 generated windows. Recorded input arrays and durations were verified before their existing matched fits were reused. Every fitting attempt completed; numerical convergence was reported separately and did not determine inclusion.')
    a.figure(assets/'component_examples.png','<b>Figure 15.</b> Saved minimum-jerk fits for subject01: the first recorded query job and first seed-42 generated job in identifier order for CVAE n=8 and VAE n=8. The black curve is the input speed (recorded or generated), blue is the norm of the fitted vector sum, and dashed curves are component speeds. Dashed magnitudes need not add to blue. The vertical dotted line marks the movement endpoint; later input speed is padded with zeros. No optimizer was rerun to choose these illustrations.',78*mm)
    a.h('10.3 Component-distribution endpoints',True)
    a.p('Five continuous descriptors were compared using KS: normalized fit error; first-component duration; first-component displacement amplitude; the fraction of total component amplitude assigned to later components; and mean overlap percentage. Components were sorted by fitted onset. Amplitude was the norm of the displacement vector, not peak speed. A one-component fit had zero secondary-amplitude fraction and zero mean overlap.')
    a.p('For adjacent components, overlap was the shared interval from the second onset to the first end divided by the interval from the first onset to the second end, multiplied by 100 and clipped to [0,100]. The implementation then averaged adjacent-pair values. This operational definition was retained for both recorded and generated fits.')
    a.p('Selected-count distributions over one to four components were compared by total variation and Jensen-Shannon divergence (base-two logarithms). Total variation sums the absolute differences in category proportions and divides by two. JSD averages each distribution\'s divergence from their equal-weight mixture. BIC-selected counts provided two additional sensitivity endpoints.')
    equation(a,assets,'tv')
    a.page();a.h('10.4 Fidelity and sensitivity results',True)
    names={'ks_mj_fit_error':'Fit error KS','ks_mj_first_duration_s':'First duration KS','ks_mj_first_amplitude':'First amplitude KS','ks_mj_secondary_amplitude_fraction':'Secondary amplitude fraction KS','ks_mj_mean_overlap_pct':'Overlap KS','count_total_variation':'Count total variation','count_jsd':'Count JSD','count_total_variation_bic':'BIC-count total variation','count_jsd_bic':'BIC-count JSD'}
    rows=[['Discrepancy','CVAE n=3','CVAE n=8','VAE n=8','Reference 95th pct.']]
    cv=e['components'].set_index('latent_dim');uv=e['uvae_components'].set_index('metric');ref=e['component_sampling'].query('latent_dim == 8').set_index('metric')
    for k,label in names.items():rows.append([label,number(cv.loc[3,k]),number(cv.loc[8,k]),number(uv.loc[k,'uvae8_mean']),number(ref.loc[k,'reference_95']) if k in ref.index else '-'])
    a.table(rows,'<b>Table 16.</b> Participant-balanced matched component discrepancies. The reference is the 95th percentile of an empirical resampling distribution, not a population confidence interval or formal acceptance threshold.',[60*mm,25*mm,25*mm,25*mm,31*mm])
    a.figure(assets/'component_counts.png','<b>Figure 16.</b> Matched selected-count proportions, averaging within participants and then equally across participants, with seed averaging for generated movements. Aggregate similarity can coexist with participant-specific discrepancies; Table 16 computes distances within participants first.',66*mm)
    a.p('Matching the procedure reduced the historical CVAE count discrepancy (approximately 0.353/0.355 at n=3/8) to 0.214/0.232. The remaining mismatch was not eliminated. Each of the seven principal discrepancies exceeded its empirical reference\'s 95th percentile for both CVAE capacities and VAE n=8. The reference drew independent resamples from each participant\'s recorded query distribution at the observed query size and a generated sample size of ten. Five hundred replicates each averaged three seed-like draws and then participants. It accounts for this finite-sample discrepancy conditional on the empirical records, not fitting, training or population uncertainty.')
    a.p('None of nine paired n=3 versus n=8 CVAE component comparisons survived BH correction. VAE n=8 did not demonstrate an improvement over CVAE n=8 on these endpoints either: all nine adjusted p-values were 0.8643. Better 11-feature generation distances therefore did not establish better minimum-jerk component fidelity.')
    a.page();a.h('10.5 Optimizer convergence and alternative assumptions',True)
    a.p('All 4,056 selected fits in the matched recorded/CVAE comparison converged, although 505 fits had at least one nonconverged alternative candidate order. For VAE n=8, 839 of 840 selected fits converged and 734 had every candidate order converge. The reported comparison retained the declared error-based selection without excluding nonconverged candidates.')
    rows=[['Subset','Higher-budget fits','Same selected count','Selected fits converged']]
    for _,r in e['component_sensitivity'].iterrows():rows.append(['Recorded' if r.kind=='recorded' else 'CVAE generated',int(r.n),f'{round(r.count_agreement*r.n)}/{int(r.n)}',f'{int(r.high_budget_selected_converged_n)}/{int(r.n)}'])
    d=e['uvae_fit_diagnostics'];rows.append(['VAE n=8 generated',d['sensitivity_fits'],f'{d["sensitivity_count_agreement"]}/{d["sensitivity_fits"]}',f'{d["sensitivity_selected_converged"]}/{d["sensitivity_fits"]}'])
    a.table(rows,'<b>Table 17.</b> Fixed higher-budget checks using four restarts and at most 700 evaluations. Selection used the first two query trials or first two seed-42 generated samples per participant, rather than selecting difficult fits after viewing their outcomes.',[58*mm,33*mm,37*mm,38*mm])
    a.p('One additional VAE-generated fit whose selected candidate had not converged was separately rerun with the higher budget. It converged with the same two-component count and nearly unchanged normalized error (0.03781723 to 0.03781690). This was a targeted diagnostic, separate from the fixed subset; the original fixed-budget score remained in the reported comparison.')
    a.p('Earlier assumption sensitivities used two randomly selected recorded trials per participant (56 total, fixed seed 20260824). Changing the filter cutoff to 5 Hz retained the error-selected count in 29/56 cases (51.8%). Changing both the minimum duration and indexed onset lower-bound step to 167 ms retained it in 31/56 (55.4%). The corresponding BIC-count agreement was 34/56 and 1/56. These historical runs used the earlier processing scheme, so they should not be combined with the matched results as a factorial experiment under one common pipeline.')
    question(a,'Which aspects of the decomposition are scientifically meaningful to compare here: count, amplitude, overlap, fit error, or another descriptor? Should minimum-jerk fidelity remain a secondary diagnostic in this project?')


def events(a,assets,e):
    a.page();a.h('11 Event timing and retained early movement')
    a.p('The interpretation of initiation time depends on the event chosen as time zero. The current model begins at target motion, while marker 5 denotes target appearance. In the available recordings, the appearance-to-motion interval ranged from 0.20 to 0.50 s, with median 0.35 s. Movement during this earlier interval remains in the recordings but is outside the modeled window.')
    a.p('A dedicated event audit examined all 4,732 retained trials without removing or relabeling them. There were 357 zero-initiation labels (7.5%). In every such trial, filtered 3D speed already exceeded the onset threshold for the required three frames at target motion; none of these labels resulted from a failed onset search. Filtered planar speed showed at least 50 ms of continuous pre-motion threshold exceedance in 109 of those trials, and raw planar speed did so in 100.')
    a.figure(assets/'event_examples.png','<b>Figure 17.</b> Recorded raw and filtered planar speed around target-motion onset. The three examples were selected by proximity to the median pre-motion threshold duration within their stated groups. Dashed vertical lines mark target motion; horizontal dotted lines mark the speed threshold. The modeled onset rule uses 3D speed, so planar plots illustrate rather than exactly reproduce that rule.',119*mm)
    a.p('Early movement was retained as behavior of interest. Zero-phase filtering can spread a change backward in time, and raw and filtered checks differ. The audit therefore describes threshold behavior without assigning its cause. The 357 zero labels contributed 74.6% of the centered squared variation in log(initiation + 0.001); that statistic is target variation, not residual loss or a measured explanation for the timing errors.')
    question(a,'Should the modeled interval begin at target appearance to include evidence gathering or movement before target motion? Please confirm the event alignment and whether the final tracker sample is the intended interception endpoint. A change in the modeling window would require a matched preprocessing and model reevaluation.')


def dashboard(a,assets,e):
    a.page();a.h('12 Interactive dashboard as the project deliverable')
    a.p('The model comparisons were translated into an interactive dashboard for exploring learned movement representations, generated trajectories, predicted durations and feature distributions. The interface exposes the tested families so that the choice of model can follow the purpose of an exploration. It uses frozen reference models and precomputed evaluation artifacts; moving a slider does not train a new model.')
    a.figure(assets/'dashboard_workflow.png','<b>Figure 18.</b> Main dashboard controls and outputs. A latent coordinate change produces a model-dependent movement; distribution sampling adds the shared training-derived variability around the selected center.',64*mm)
    a.table([['Reference family','Available dimensions','Use of task controls'],['CVAE','2, 3, 4, 8','Encoder and decoder are conditional'],['CAE','3, 8','Encoder and decoder are conditional'],['VAE','3, 8','Inactive; outputs model the condition mixture'],['Spline + PCA','2, 3, 4, 8','Timing regression only; shape depends on PCA scores']],'<b>Table 18.</b> Supported dashboard references. The condition-only Ridge remains an evaluation baseline; it has no personal latent fingerprint and is not a live reference model.',[40*mm,40*mm,86*mm])
    a.p('The default is VAE n=8 because it had the lowest cohort-average KS, energy and MMD-squared generation discrepancies in the tested matrix. The choice is endpoint-specific and based on the evaluated cohort, with no separate post-selection validation. CVAE n=3 remains available for the three-number, explicitly conditional setting; spline and CAE provide useful alternatives for reconstruction. No n=16 result is available from the matched study.')
    a.p('Live references use fold 0 and neural seed 42. The user can choose the training-population center or one of that fold\'s enrolled test-participant context centroids, then move coordinates in units of training-code standard deviation. The distribution panel samples 25 to 500 latent points, default 100, using a user-selected random seed and the training covariance. Conditional generation uses the selected task controls; the unconditional model ignores them.')
    a.p('The held-out validation section instead displays the exported evaluations across all four folds at seed 42, using 120 generated samples per participant. Summary score cards use the study\'s available training seeds. These displays answer different questions and are labeled accordingly. A live center-decoded curve should not be presented as a recorded trial or as a quantitative validation result.')
    a.h('12.1 Scope of the deliverable',True)
    a.p('The dashboard includes trajectory, speed and illustrative minimum-jerk views, feature distributions, held-out comparisons, latent associations, study diagnostics and report downloads. Its live decomposition uses one restart and at most 300 evaluations; this exploratory fit does not replace the matched offline component evaluation. Successful software checks establish that the selected model and assets load and execute; they do not validate an interpretation of the sliders as cognitive strategies.')
    question(a,'For the next stage, is the priority a strict three-number representation, the best observed marginal-distribution fidelity, or reliable control by experimental condition? The current results support different model choices for these aims.')
    a.page();a.h('12.2 Additional evaluated capacities and evidence boundaries',True)
    rows=[['Model','n','Trajectory MSE','Initiation MAE (ms)','Movement MAE (ms)','Mean KS','Energy','MMD squared']]
    for n in [2,4]:
        for f in ['spline_pca','cvae']:
            r=e['means'].query('model_family == @f and latent_dim == @n').iloc[0]
            rows.append([LABELS[f],n,number(r.trajectory_mse,4),number(r.initiation_time_mae_ms,2),number(r.movement_time_mae_ms,2),number(r.mean_ks),number(r.energy_distance),number(r.mmd_rbf)])
    a.table(rows,'<b>Table 19.</b> Additional capacities in the original CVAE/spline sweep. These complement the n=3 and n=8 matched comparisons; CAE and VAE were not evaluated at n=2 or n=4.',[28*mm,8*mm,25*mm,25*mm,25*mm,18*mm,18*mm,19*mm])
    a.h('Condition-only population generation',True)
    a.p('The condition-only Ridge baseline also generated stochastic movements without a personal code. Separate Ridge models predicted coordinates and standardized log-durations from the five task values, with validation-selected alpha from 0, 0.01, 0.1, 1, 10 and 100. Generation added a paired trajectory/timing residual from a training trial in the same start-category and side stratum, falling back to the full training pool if needed. Continuous speed entered the regression rather than requiring an exact residual match.')
    a.p('Its cohort-average generation discrepancies were mean KS 0.277264, energy 0.818722 and MMD squared 0.125854. These competitive marginal scores, despite much poorer trajectory reconstruction, further show why generation and compression require separate evaluation. The baseline has no participant centroid; it cannot itself demonstrate that a personal fingerprint contributes information.')
    a.p('The results form a sequence of distinct tests: compression of observed paths; generation of query-feature distributions from personal context; replacement of that context to test its contribution; prediction of timing and behavioural summaries; and secondary checks of conditioning, component fitting and event semantics. An advantage in one test does not establish an advantage in the others.')
    a.p('The audit reproduced the saved neural outputs, checked condition routing and participant separation, and corrected confirmed implementation defects in auxiliary code. All 96 frozen neural checkpoint hashes remained unchanged. The model matrix used four declared participant folds and three neural seeds; it did not enumerate all possible participant splits or test every architecture and capacity. No controlled learning-curve experiment was performed to attribute a limitation to participant count.')
    a.p('The original 56-test benchmark remains in the evidence archive. This draft uses the expanded exploratory all-pairs comparison (140 tests). Separate BH families were used for that comparison, CVAE fingerprint replacements (12), VAE fingerprint replacements (6), timing-head sensitivities (8), condition-feature comparisons (44), CVAE component-capacity contrasts (9) and VAE-CVAE component contrasts (9). Holm sensitivity over the expanded 140 and the two condition-trajectory contrasts is stated where relevant. These adjustments were not a single correction over every exploratory analysis.')
    a.p('Training folds overlap. The historical character-sum participant seed scheme also maps multiple participant identifiers to the same sampling seed, creating shared simulation randomness. Neither multiple-testing adjustment nor repeated training seeds removes those dependence limits. They should remain explicit when interpreting the strength and scope of the evidence.')
    a.p('This detailed version assembles the methods, results and domain questions for laboratory review. The final introduction, abstract and discussion will be written around the agreed scientific objective and the results retained with the advisors. The eventual eight-page main paper can point to the complete supplementary evidence without presenting every diagnostic as a separate primary finding.')
